## Summary

The bug was in `consume_restricted_single_text_expression` in `crates/biome_html_parser/src/lexer/mod.rs`. When lexing a Svelte `#each` key expression, the parser uses `RestrictedExpressionStopAt::ClosingParen`, for which `matches_punct(')')` returns `true`.

The closing-paren match arm was guarded by `!kind.matches_branch(current)`:

```rust
_ if is_closing_paren(current) && !kind.matches_punct(current) => { ... }
```

For the `ClosingParen` mode this guard evaluated to `false` for every `)`, so `)` was **never** treated as a bracket-balancing token. A nested `)` (e.g. from `toString()` or `key(number)`) fell through to the default arm and was consumed without decrementing `brackets_stack`, which left the stack unbalanced — and the outer delimiting `)` was then also swallowed into the literal, producing parse errors and a corrupted key node.

**The fix:** drop the guard so the closing-paren arm balances nested parens against `brackets_stack` and breaks only when a closing paren is reached at the top level (stack == 0):

```rust
_ if is_closing_paren(current) => {
    if brackets_stack == 0 {
        break;          // top-level delimiter → stop
    } else {
        brackets_stack -= 1;   // nested close → balance
        self.advance(1)
    }
}
```

This is a no-op for the `AsOrComma` and `ThenOrCatch` stop modes (their `matches_punct` never matches a paren, so the guard was always `true` there anyway), so only the `ClosingParen` case changes.

Result: `number.toString()` and `key(number)` are each captured as the full `HTML_LITERAL` content of the `HtmlTextExpression`, with the outer parens preserved as separate `L_PAREN`/`R_PAREN` tokens in the `SvelteEachKey` node, and zero diagnostics.

Per the instructions, I only modified source (`lexer/mod.rs`) and left the `tests/` directory untouched for the verification harness to patch.

